(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["brahma"]={"slug":"brahma","name":"Brahma","deva":"ब्रह्म","note":"","intro":["<strong>Muktikā 11 of 108.</strong> A short <em>saṃnyāsa</em> Upaniṣad in mixed prose and verse, best known for a single argument: that the two outward marks of the twice-born brahmin — the <strong><em>yajñopavīta</em></strong> (sacred thread) and the <strong><em>śikhā</em></strong> (hair-tuft) — signify realities that can be possessed directly, and that whoever possesses them may lay the outward signs down. Around that argument the text places a Māṇḍūkya-derived account of four states of consciousness in four bodily stations, a sweeping negation of every category by which a person is religiously and socially identified, and a closing anthology of verses on the Self drawn largely from the Śvetāśvatara.","Every claim below carries a provenance tag. This edition distinguishes what is transmitted, what is translated, what is attested in a published source, and what is my own reading."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Brahma Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>ब्रह्म उपनिषत्</strong> — <em>\"The Upaniṣad of Brahman\"</em> (Brahmopaniṣad)","<strong>Muktikā 11 of 108.</strong> A short <em>saṃnyāsa</em> Upaniṣad in mixed prose and verse, best known for a single argument: that the two outward marks of the twice-born brahmin — the <strong><em>yajñopavīta</em></strong> (sacred thread) and the <strong><em>śikhā</em></strong> (hair-tuft) — signify realities that can be possessed directly, and that whoever possesses them may lay the outward signs down. Around that argument the text places a Māṇḍūkya-derived account of four states of consciousness in four bodily stations, a sweeping negation of every category by which a person is religiously and socially identified, and a closing anthology of verses on the Self drawn largely from the Śvetāśvatara."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"How to read this edition","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Every claim below carries a provenance tag. This edition distinguishes what is transmitted, what is translated, what is attested in a published source, and what is my own reading.","<table><tr><td>Tag</td><td>Meaning</td></tr><tr><td><strong>⟦TEXT⟧</strong></td><td>The transmitted Sanskrit, with variants recorded and their witnesses named</td></tr><tr><td><strong>⟦TRANS⟧</strong></td><td>A translation made for this edition, with the pada analysis shown so it can be checked</td></tr><tr><td><strong>⟦CITED⟧</strong></td><td>Quoted or closely reported from a source actually consulted, named with page</td></tr><tr><td><strong>⟦COMM⟧</strong></td><td>The commentary of Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin, as rendered in the Adyar edition</td></tr><tr><td><strong>⟦ED⟧</strong></td><td>My own analysis. Not attributed to any scholar, and to be weighed as the reading of an editor, not an authority</td></tr><tr><td><strong>⟦UNCERTAIN⟧</strong></td><td>Unresolved. Competing options given with the reasons each is held</td></tr></table>","<strong>Nothing in this edition is attributed to a commentator whose text was not consulted.</strong> Where a source is silent on a passage, the entry says so rather than supplying a plausible substitute. This is a change from the first draft of this volume; see <strong>Revision Notes</strong> at the end for the list of corrections made and why."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sources actually consulted","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Siglum</td><td>Source</td><td>Nature</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E</strong></td><td>`brahma_upan.itx`, ITRANS e-text, sanskritdocuments.org, tr. Anshuman Pandey, rev. 19 Aug 2018</td><td>The base Sanskrit text of this edition. Heavily corrupted; see Textual Notes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ad</strong></td><td><em>The Saṃnyāsa Upaniṣad-s</em>, tr. <strong>A. A. Ramanathan</strong>, Adyar Library Series vol. 104, 1978, pp. 166–171 — <em>\"translated into English based on the commentary of Upaniṣad Brahmayogin\"</em></td><td>The only access here to <strong>UBY's commentary</strong>. Numbers the text continuously as <strong>23 verses</strong> and <strong>omits khaṇḍa 1</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ai</strong></td><td><strong>K. Nārāyaṇasvāmi Aiyar</strong>, <em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em>, Madras 1914, pp. 106–109</td><td>Full English translation including khaṇḍa 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>De</strong></td><td><strong>Paul Deussen</strong>, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (1897), Eng. tr. V. M. Bedekar &amp; G. B. Palsule, <em>Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda</em>, Vol. 2, Delhi 1980, pp. 725–732</td><td>Translation with a text-critical introduction; the most rigorous witness of the three</td></tr></table>","<strong>Not consulted, and therefore not cited anywhere below:</strong> the Sanskrit <em>Dīpikā</em> of Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin in the original; <strong>Nārāyaṇa's</strong> commentary and <strong>Śaṅkarānanda's</strong> commentary (both named by De as principal witnesses to this text); Olivelle's <em>Saṃnyāsa Upaniṣads</em> (1992); Schrader's <em>Minor Upaniṣads</em> (1912). Where these would matter, the entry says so.","<strong>A note on what this means.</strong> De reports that Śaṅkarānanda's commentary <strong>ignores khaṇḍa 1 entirely</strong>, and that Nārāyaṇa knows of editions beginning at khaṇḍa 2 without endorsing them (De p. 725, citing Nārāyaṇa p. 239.10). Ad, following UBY, likewise begins at khaṇḍa 2. Any reading of khaṇḍa 1 therefore rests on E and Ai alone, and both are working from a badly damaged text."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The recensions, and a numbering concordance","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This text does not exist in one form. Four differences are attested:","1. <strong>Khaṇḍa 1 present or absent.</strong> E prints it, noting that \"many publications do not include the first verse above.\" Ai translates it. <strong>Ad omits it.</strong> De: \"This whole section is missing in most of the manuscripts\" (p. 725).","2. <strong>De reports a Telugu edition carrying two entirely different recensions</strong> — \"without the first part as <em>Brahma Upaniṣad</em>, and with it as <em>Parabrahma Upaniṣad</em>\" (p. 725 n. 2). The Parabrahma Upaniṣad is <strong>Muktikā 78</strong>, a separate text in the canon.","3. <strong>The <em>sandhyā</em> verses</strong> (E khaṇḍa 4; Ai p. 109) are <strong>absent from Ad</strong>, which passes directly from the state-map to <em>yato vāco nivartante</em>.","4. <strong>Verse division.</strong> De: \"the divisions in the Calcutta and both Poona editions (with the commentary of Nārāyaṇa and that of Śaṅkarānanda) are partly confused and partly of no use whatever\" — his own four-part division \"originates partly from us\" (p. 725 n. 2).","<strong>Concordance</strong> (E khaṇḍa/section as used here ↔ Ad verse number):","<table><tr><td>This edition</td><td>Content</td><td>Ad verse</td></tr><tr><td>1.1–1.6</td><td>Śaunaka's question; the four similes</td><td><em>omitted</em></td></tr><tr><td>2.1–2.2</td><td>Four stations; four states; one Person</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2.3</td><td>The negation</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>2.4</td><td>The heart-space</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>3.1</td><td>Deities in the heart; the threefold cord; investiture mantra</td><td>4–5</td></tr><tr><td>3.2</td><td>Discard the outer thread; <em>sūcanāt sūtram</em></td><td>6–7</td></tr><tr><td>3.3</td><td>Gems on a thread; purity; the true thread-wearers</td><td>8–11</td></tr><tr><td>3.4</td><td>The flame-tuft</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>4.1</td><td>Who keeps the thread; brahminhood entire</td><td>13–14</td></tr><tr><td>—</td><td>The thread as sacrifice</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>4.2</td><td>The one God (= Śvet. 6.11–12)</td><td>16–17</td></tr><tr><td>4.3</td><td>The churning (= Śvet. 1.14–15)</td><td>18–19</td></tr><tr><td>4.4</td><td>Spider; heart-lotus; state-map</td><td>20–21</td></tr><tr><td>4.5</td><td><em>Sandhyā</em></td><td><em>absent from Ad</em></td></tr><tr><td>4.6</td><td>Where speech turns back; ghee in milk (= Śvet. 1.16)</td><td>22–23</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Veda, class, and date","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Question</td><td>Evidence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Which Veda?</strong></td><td>E's colophon: <em>ity <strong>atharvavede</strong> brahmopaniṣat samāptā</em>. <strong>Ad, following UBY, assigns it to the Ārṣṇa-(Kṛṣṇa-)Yajurveda</strong> (p. 166). The Muktikā also places it in the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda. ⟦UNCERTAIN⟧ — the colophon and the commentarial tradition disagree, and this edition does not resolve it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Which class?</strong></td><td>A <strong><em>saṃnyāsa</em> Upaniṣad</strong>. This is not inference: <strong>Ad prints it in the Saṃnyāsa collection</strong>, and <strong>De groups it under \"c. Saṃnyāsa Upaniṣads\"</strong> (p. 725), describing it as \"a transition from the Vedānta and the Yoga-Upaniṣads to those texts which see the highest goal in the Saṃnyāsa.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Muktikā number</strong></td><td><strong>11</strong>. ⟦CITED⟧ Ad p. 166: \"the eleventh among the one hundred and eight Upaniṣad-s.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Structure</strong></td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 725: four parts, \"two in prose of an ore-archaic bearing and two in verse which show a more modern character in respect to the form and content.\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Speakers</strong></td><td><strong>Śaunaka</strong> questions <strong>Pippalāda the Āṅgirasa</strong>. ⟦ED⟧ Both names are borrowed — Śaunaka from Muṇḍaka 1.1.3, Pippalāda from the Praśna — and the pairing looks like a deliberate claim to Atharvan descent. De notes the contrast with the Atharvaśikha's use of the same figures (p. 725).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Date</strong></td><td>⟦ED⟧ Composite and late: it quotes Śvetāśvatara and Taittirīya, and presupposes an established renunciate order. No firm date is available and none is asserted here.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha","title":"","sa":["ॐ भद्रं कर्णेभिः शृणुयाम देवाः । भद्रं पश्येमाक्षभिर्यजत्राः ।","स्थिरैरङ्गैस्तुष्टुवांसस्तनूभिः । व्यशेम देवहितं यदायुः ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ śṛṇuyāma devāḥ | bhadraṃ paśyemākṣabhir yajatrāḥ |","sthirair aṅgais tuṣṭuvāṃsas tanūbhiḥ | vyaśema devahitaṃ yad āyuḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"May we hear what is auspicious with our ears, O gods; may we see what is auspicious with our eyes, O worshipful ones. With steady limbs and bodies, praising, may we enjoy the life allotted us by the gods.\"","para":"","coms":[["Editor","E transmits no śānti-pāṭha with this text. The Atharvan bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ is given here on the strength of E's colophon; editions following the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurvedic attribution prefix saha nāvavatu instead. This is an editorial supplement, not part of the transmitted text."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"The Text","u":[{"ref":"The Text","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each passage: <strong>⟦TEXT⟧</strong> Devanāgarī and IAST with variants → <strong>pada gloss</strong> → <strong>⟦TRANS⟧</strong> literal translation → <strong>⟦CITED⟧</strong> the published translations where they differ → <strong>⟦COMM⟧</strong> UBY where available → <strong>⟦ED⟧</strong> editorial comment.","<em>Grammar abbreviations:</em> m./f./n. = gender; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./pf./impv./opt. = tense-mood; pp. = past participle; abs. = absolutive. † = corrupt in E."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 1 — Śaunaka's Question and the Similes of the Living Self","u":[{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 1","title":"Śaunaka's Question and the Similes of the Living Self","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Editor","The most damaged part of a damaged text, and the part with the weakest attestation: absent from most manuscripts, ignored by Śaṅkarānanda, omitted by Ad and UBY. It is translated here because Ai and De both translate it and because its four similes supply the four states that khaṇḍa 2 presupposes — but <strong>no argument in this edition rests on khaṇḍa 1</strong>, and readers should treat its detail as provisional."]],"notes":[],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1.1","title":"The question","sa":["ॐ शौनको ह वै महाशालोऽङ्गिरसं भगवन्तं पिप्पलादमपृच्छत् ।","दिव्ये ब्रह्मपुरे सम्प्रतिष्ठिता भवन्ति कथं सृजन्ति कस्यैष महिमा बभूव","यो ह्मषे महिमा बभूव क एषः ।"],"iast":["oṃ śaunako ha vai mahāśālo 'ṅgirasaṃ bhagavantaṃ pippalādam apṛcchat |","divye brahmapure sampratiṣṭhitā bhavanti kathaṃ sṛjanti kasyaiṣa mahimā babhūva","yo hmaṣe† mahimā babhūva ka eṣaḥ |"],"words":[["śaunakaḥ ha vai mahāśālaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Śaunaka, the great householder"],["āṅgirasaṃ bhagavantaṃ pippalādam","m. acc. sg.","the blessed Pippalāda, of Aṅgiras' line"],["apṛcchat","impf. 3 sg.","asked"],["divye brahma-pure","n. loc. sg.","in the divine city of Brahman"],["sampratiṣṭhitāḥ bhavanti","m. nom. pl. pp. + pres.","are (they) established"],["kathaṃ sṛjanti","adv. + pres. 3 pl.","how do they send forth?"],["kasya eṣaḥ mahimā babhūva","gen. + m. nom. + pf.","whose is this power that has come to be?"],["yaḥ … mahimā babhūva ka eṣaḥ","rel. + m. nom.","who is he that has become this power?"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. Śaunaka the great householder asked the blessed Pippalāda, of Aṅgiras' line: 'In the divine city of Brahman, how are they established? How do they send forth? Whose is this power that has arisen? And who is he that has become this power?'\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 726 renders four distinct questions: \"In the divine, lovely Brahman city [in the body] how are they (the gods of the vital organs) established? And how do they break forth [outwards]? And from whom originates this power of theirs? And who is he, who has become this power of theirs?\""],["Cited","Ai p. 106 does not preserve a four-fold structure."],["Uncertain reading","E's <em>yo hmaṣe</em> is meaningless as transmitted. De's rendering presupposes something like <em>yo 'sya mahimā babhūva</em>, \"who has become this power of theirs\" — which yields a fourth question and a coherent clause. This edition follows De. <em>(In the first draft of this volume I marked the clause unrecoverable and guessed at dittography; De's reconstruction is better and is adopted.)</em>"],["Editor","The subject of <em>sampratiṣṭhitāḥ</em> is unexpressed. De supplies \"the gods of the vital organs,\" which fits the answer given (<em>prāṇo hy eṣa ātmā</em> and, below, <em>prāṇa-devatās tāḥ sarvā nāḍyaḥ</em>). The <em>divya brahmapura</em> is the body as a walled city, the image of Chāndogya 8.1 and Kaṭha 2.2.1."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"1.2","title":"Prāṇa is the Self","sa":["तस्मै स होवाच ब्रह्मविद्यं वरिष्ठाम् । प्राणो ह्येष आत्मा ।","आत्मनो महिमा बभूव देवानामायुः स देवानां निधनमनिधनं","दिव्ये ब्रह्मपुरे विरजं निष्कलं शुभ्रमक्षरं यद्ब्रह्म विभाति ।"],"iast":["tasmai sa hovāca brahmavidyāṃ variṣṭhām | prāṇo hy eṣa ātmā |","ātmano mahimā babhūva devānām āyuḥ sa devānāṃ nidhanam anidhanaṃ","divye brahmapure virajaṃ niṣkalaṃ śubhram akṣaraṃ yad brahma vibhāti |"],"words":[["prāṇaḥ hi eṣaḥ ātmā","m. nom.","for this prāṇa is the Self"],["ātmanaḥ mahimā babhūva","gen. + m. nom. + pf.","from the Self the power has arisen"],["devānām āyuḥ","gen. pl. + n.","the life of the gods"],["devānāṃ nidhanam anidhanam","n.","the gods' disappearance and non-disappearance"],["virajaṃ niṣkalaṃ śubhram akṣaram","n.","dustless, partless, pure, imperishable"]],"lit":"\"To him he declared the most excellent knowledge of Brahman: 'This prāṇa is the Self. From the Self the power arose. He is the life of the gods; he is the gods' disappearance and non-disappearance. In the divine city of Brahman shines that Brahman — dustless, partless, pure, imperishable.'\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 726:<strong> \"It is the Prāṇa, is the Ātman; from the Ātman originates their power; he is the vital power of the gods [of the vital organs]; he is the [place of] disappearance and appearance of gods.\" De cross-refers the four epithets to </strong>Muṇḍaka 2.2.9."],["Uncertain reading","<em>nidhanam anidhanam</em>: De reads the pair as \"disappearance and appearance\" — i.e. the Self is where the gods set and rise. An alternative construal takes <em>anidhanam</em> as an epithet of the Self (\"he is the gods' death, himself deathless\"). ⟦ED⟧ De's is the more economical reading of the pair and is followed here; the alternative was preferred in this volume's first draft without argument, and I no longer think it is better supported."],["Editor","<em>Prāṇo hy eṣa ātmā</em> sounds like the archaic <em>prāṇa</em>-monism of Bṛhadāraṇyaka 1.3 and Kauṣītaki 3, but the following clause transfers the power to the Ātman (<em>ātmano mahimā</em>), which suggests <em>prāṇa</em> functions here as the name under which the Self operates the organs rather than as the ultimate principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"1.3","title":"Bees and spider","sa":["स नियच्छति मधुकरराजानं माक्षिकवदिति । यथा माक्षीकैकेन तन्तुना","जालं वक्षिपति तेनापकर्षति तथैवैष प्राणो यदा याति संसृष्टमाकृष्य ।","प्राणदेवतास्ताः सर्वा नाड्यः ।"],"iast":["sa niyacchati madhukara-rājānaṃ mākṣikavad† iti | yathā mākṣīka ekena tantunā","jālaṃ vakṣipati† tenāpakarṣati tathaivaiṣa prāṇo yadā yāti saṃsṛṣṭam ākṛṣya |","prāṇa-devatās tāḥ sarvā nāḍyaḥ |"],"words":[],"lit":"\"He controls them as the bees the bee-king. And as the spider spreads out its web by a single thread and by that draws it back, so this prāṇa, when it goes, having drawn in what was spread out. All those channels have prāṇa as their deity.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 726 n. 1:<strong> the bee-image is \"according to </strong>Praśna 2.4<strong>, with which passage <em>makṣikāvat</em> has to be read. The corruption is due to the following <em>yathā makṣikā</em>.\" De renders the second image with \"the female fly-destroyer [the spider]\" and refers it to </strong>Muṇḍaka 1.1.7."],["Editor","E's <em>mākṣīka</em> (\"bee, fly\") in the second simile is a slide from the first; the verse form at 4.4 reads <em>ūrṇanābhi</em>, \"spider,\" correctly. <em>Vakṣipati</em> is for <em>vikṣipati</em>. De's identification of the bee-simile with Praśna 2.4 (where the vital breaths serve <em>prāṇa</em> as bees their king) supplies the point of the comparison, which is otherwise obscure."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"1.4","title":"The falcon; the stick; the child","sa":["सुष्वपे श्येनाकाशवद्यथा खं श्येनमाश्रित्य याति स्वमालयमेवं सुषुप्तो ब्रूते ।","यथैवैष देवदत्तो यष्ट्याऽपि ताड्यमानो न यत्येवमिष्टापूर्तैः शुभाशुभैर्न लिप्यते ।","यथा कुमारो निष्काम आनन्दमुपयाति तथैवैष देवदत्तः स्वप्न आनन्दमभियाति ।"],"iast":["suṣvape śyenākāśavad yathā khaṃ śyenam āśritya yāti svam ālayam evaṃ suṣupto brūte |","yathaivaiṣa devadatto yaṣṭyā 'pi tāḍyamāno na yati† evam iṣṭāpūrtaiḥ śubhāśubhair na lipyate |","yathā kumāro niṣkāma ānandam upayāti tathaivaiṣa devadattaḥ svapna ānandam abhiyāti |"],"words":[],"lit":"\"In deep sleep, as a falcon in the sky: as the falcon, taking to the sky, goes to its own nest, so is the deep sleeper — for so he reports. And as this Devadatta, though struck with a stick, does not stir, so he is not smeared by rites and public works, by good and by bad. As a child free of desire attains bliss, so this Devadatta attains bliss in sleep.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 727<strong> emends: \"as long as he is </strong>not<strong> struck with a stick or the like [read: <em>yaṣṭi-ādinā atāḍyamāna</em>, in view of </strong>Kauṣ. 4.19], does not stir about.\" This reverses the sense of E's <em>api tāḍyamāno</em>."],["Cited","De p. 727<strong> refers the falcon to </strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3.19, and supplies from the commentary that the deep sleeper \"says it [after waking up: 'I have slept well']\" — which explains <em>brūte</em>."],["Cited","Ai p. 107 places the child's bliss in <em>suṣupti</em>, not <em>svapna</em>: \"so also devadatta obtains happiness in sushupti.\" E reads <em>svapne</em>."],["Uncertain reading","Two unresolved points: whether the sleeper is unmoved <em>although</em> struck (E, Ai) or <em>because</em> unstruck (De's emendation); and whether the child-simile applies to dream (E) or deep sleep (Ai). Neither affects the sentence that matters."],["Editor","That sentence is <em>iṣṭāpūrtaiḥ śubhāśubhair na lipyate</em> — unsmeared by <em>iṣṭa</em> (sacrificial rites) and <em>pūrta</em> (public benefactions), <strong>good and bad alike</strong>. Stated here of deep sleep, where it is uncontroversial; khaṇḍa 3 will rely on it for a waking man."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"1.5","title":"The looper","sa":["भूयस्तेनैव स्वप्नाय गच्छति जलौकावत् । यथा जलौकाऽग्रमग्रं नयत्यात्मानं","नयति परं संधय । यत्परं नापरं त्यजति स जाग्रदभिधीयते ।"],"iast":["bhūyas tenaiva svapnāya gacchati jalaukāvat | yathā jalaukā 'gram agraṃ nayaty ātmānaṃ","nayati paraṃ saṃdhaya† | yat paraṃ nāparaṃ tyajati sa jāgrad abhidhīyate |"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Again by that same path he goes to dream, like a <em>jalaukā</em>. As the <em>jalaukā</em> carries itself forward tip by tip, joining a further hold, and does not let go of the nearer until it has the further — that is called waking.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Both Ai (p. 107) and De (p. 727) render <em>jalaukā</em> as \"caterpillar,\" describing a looping motion: De, \"just like a caterpillar which propels itself from leaf-edge to leaf-edge, propelling its front part and dragging the hind part behind it (read <em>saṃdhāyati aparam</em>)\"; Ai, \"It remaining on a blade of grass, first puts forward its foot on another blade in front, conveys its body to it, and having got a firm hold of it, then only leaves the former.\""],["Editor","<em>Jalaukā</em> is literally \"leech,\" but the motion described is that of a looper or measuring-worm, and the source passage <strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.3</strong> uses <em>tṛṇa-jalāyukā</em>, \"grass-leech,\" for the same creature. \"Leech\" renders the word; \"looper\" identifies the animal. <em>(The first draft of this volume rendered it \"leech\" without noting that both published translations read \"caterpillar.\")</em>"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"1.6","title":"The eight potsherds; the litany of names","sa":["यथैवैष कपालाष्टकं संनयति । तमेव स्तन इव लम्बते वेददेवयोनः ।","यत्र जाग्रति शुभाशुभं निरुक्तमस्य देवस्य स सम्प्रसारोऽन्तर्यामी खगः कर्कटकः","पुष्करः पुरुषः प्राणो हिंसा परापरं ब्रह्म आत्मा देवता वेदयति ।","य एवं वेद स परं ब्रह्म क्षेत्रज्ञमुपैति ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["yathaivaiṣa kapālāṣṭakaṃ saṃnayati | tam eva stana iva lambate vedadevayonaḥ† |","yatra jāgrati śubhāśubhaṃ niruktam asya devasya sa samprasāro 'ntaryāmī khagaḥ karkaṭakaḥ","puṣkaraḥ puruṣaḥ prāṇo [E: grāṇo] hiṃsā parāparaṃ brahma ātmā devatā vedayati |","ya evaṃ veda sa paraṃ brahma kṣetrajñam [E: dhaṃ kṣetrajñam] upaiti || 1 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As he holds together the eight potsherds, so it clings to him like a breast — he who is the source of the Vedas and the gods. Wherein, when men wake, good and evil are the expression of this god: he is the <em>samprasāra</em>, the inner controller, the bird, the crab, the lotus, the Person, the breath, harm, the higher-and-lower Brahman, the Self, the deity — thus he causes to know. He who knows thus attains the supreme Brahman, the knower of the field.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 727<strong> reads <em>kapālāṣṭaka</em> ritually: \"just as one [who wants to perform a sacrifice] keeps the eight potsherds together, similarly he bears it (the body with the organs).\" </strong>Ai p. 107 reads it anatomically: \"as this (devadatta) bears at the same time eight skulls.\""],["Cited","De p. 728 n. 1<strong> reports </strong>Nārāyaṇa's<strong> reading of the obscure clause: \"It (the deep sleep, dream, waking) hangs on him (the Ātman) like a woman's breast; he is the source of the Vedas and the gods,\" and De cross-refers <em>stana iva lambate</em> to </strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3.21. Ai p. 107 independently renders \"the source of Devas and Vedas,\" confirming the sense of E's corrupt <em>vedadevayonaḥ</em>."],["Cited","De p. 728 n. 2, on <em>karkaṭaka</em>: \"The comparison of the Ātman with a crab makes no particular meaning and is isolated in the Upaniṣad literature.\""],["Cited","Ai p. 107<strong> glosses <em>puṣkara</em> as </strong>ākāśa, not lotus; and renders <em>hiṃsā</em> as \"pain.\""],["Cited","De p. 728<strong> on <em>samprasāra</em>: \"as the origin of the world [if one does not read <em>samprasāda</em>, </strong>Chānd. 8.12.3<strong>].\" </strong>Ai p. 107: \"he is capable of great expansion.\""],["Uncertain reading","<em>samprasāra</em> is read three ways: \"origin of the world\" (De, on the transmitted spelling), \"great expansion\" (Ai), or — if emended to <em>samprasāda</em> — the \"serene one\" of deep sleep (Chāndogya 8.12.3; cf. BĀU 4.3.15). <em>(The first draft asserted the last without flagging that it requires an emendation.)</em>"],["Editor","Since De finds <em>karkaṭaka</em> meaningless and isolated, and Ai and I differ on <em>puṣkara</em> and <em>hiṃsā</em>, the litany should be treated as a list whose individual items are insecure and whose cumulative force — that one principle bears many names — is not. The closing <em>paraṃ brahma kṣetrajñam</em> is the notable phrase; De (p. 728) refers it to <strong>Muṇḍaka 3.2.1</strong>."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 2 — Four Stations, Four States; the Negation; the Heart-Space","u":[{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 2","title":"Four Stations, Four States; the Negation; the Heart-Space","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Editor","Where the attested text begins. Ad and UBY start here; so, De reports, do most manuscripts."]],"notes":[],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2.1","title":"The four stations and the four quarters","sa":["अथास्य पुरुषस्य चत्वारि स्थानानि भवन्ति नाभिर्हृदयं कण्ठं मूर्धेति ।","तत्र चतुष्पादं ब्रह्म विभाति । जागरितं स्वप्नं सुषुप्तं तुरीयमिति ।","जागरिते ब्रह्मा स्वप्ने विष्णुः सुषुप्तौ रुद्रस्तुरीयं परमाक्षरम् ।"],"iast":["athāsya puruṣasya catvāri sthānāni bhavanti nābhir hṛdayaṃ kaṇṭhaṃ mūrdhā [E: bhūrdhā] iti |","tatra catuṣpādaṃ brahma vibhāti | jāgaritaṃ svapnaṃ suṣuptaṃ turīyam iti |","jāgarite brahmā svapne viṣṇuḥ suṣuptau rudras turīyaṃ paramākṣaram |"],"words":[["catvāri sthānāni bhavanti","n. nom. pl. + pres.","there are four stations"],["nābhiḥ hṛdayaṃ kaṇṭhaṃ mūrdhā","nom./acc.","navel, heart, throat, head"],["tatra catuṣ-pādaṃ brahma vibhāti","n. + pres.","there the four-quartered Brahman shines"],["jāgaritaṃ svapnaṃ suṣuptaṃ turīyam","n. nom.","waking, dream, deep sleep, the fourth"],["jāgarite brahmā … suṣuptau rudraḥ","loc. + nom.","in waking Brahmā … in deep sleep Rudra"],["turīyaṃ parama-akṣaram","n. nom.","the fourth, the supreme Imperishable"]],"lit":"\"Now of this Person there are four stations: navel, heart, throat, head. There the four-quartered Brahman shines: waking, dream, deep sleep, the fourth. In waking, Brahmā; in dream, Viṣṇu; in deep sleep, Rudra; the fourth is the supreme Imperishable.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 725 (introduction), explicitly:<strong> the second part \"mentions the four physical seats and </strong>(without assigning to these) the four states of the Ātman.\" His translation (p. 728) keeps them unpaired: \"There are four dwellings of this Puruṣa; the navel, the heart, the neck and the head. In them the Brahman shines with its four quarters which are: the waking, the dream, the deep sleep, the Turīyam.\""],["Cited","Ad p. 166 likewise lists without pairing: \"the Puruṣa can be realized in the four places … the navel, heart, throat and the head. There Brahman consisting of four <em>pāda</em>-s shines [as] god Brahmā in the waking state…\""],["Cited","Ai p. 107 the same: \"The Puruṣa has four seats — navel, heart, neck, and head. There Brahman with the four feet specially shines. Those feet are jāgrata, svapna, suṣupti, and turya.\""],["Editor","Correction of record.<strong> The first draft of this volume paired the lists item by item — navel→waking, heart→dream, throat→deep sleep, head→<em>turīya</em> — and then built an extended discussion on the \"discrepancy\" between that map and the one at 4.4. </strong>The text makes no such pairing here, and none of the three published translations supplies one.<strong> What is paired here is <em>states with deities</em>, not <em>states with places</em>. The pairing of states with places occurs only at </strong>4.4<strong>, and there the first member is <em>netra</em> (eye), not <em>nābhi</em> (navel) — which De notes explicitly (p. 726: \"the four places of Brahman which are here paralleled with the four states, for which purpose, however, <em>nābhi</em> has been replaced by <em>netram</em>\"). There is therefore </strong>one state-map in this text, not two, and the only difference between the two lists of places is <em>nābhi</em> / <em>netra</em>."],["Editor","What remains notable is that the three deities are distributed over the three states and <strong>the fourth takes no deity</strong> but the <em>paramākṣara</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"2.2","title":"One Person under several names","sa":["आदित्यश्च विष्णुश्चेश्वरश्च स पुरुषः स प्राणः स जीवः सोऽग्निः सेश्वरश्च","जाग्रत्तेषां मध्ये यत्परं ब्रह्म विभाति ।"],"iast":["ādityaś ca viṣṇuś ceśvaraś ca sa puruṣaḥ sa prāṇaḥ sa jīvaḥ so 'gniḥ seśvaraś ca","jāgrat teṣāṃ madhye yat paraṃ brahma vibhāti |"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The Sun, and Viṣṇu, and the Lord: he is the Person, he is prāṇa, he is the <em>jīva</em>, he is Agni and the Lord — awake in the midst of them, that supreme Brahman shines.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 728:<strong> \"This is Āditya, Viṣṇu and Īśvara, this is Puruṣa, this is Prāṇa, this Jīva, and this is the god-filled fire, which </strong>keeps guard<strong> (</strong>Praśna 4.3) in the Brahman-city of the body; in those (four dwellings) the highest Brahman shines.\" De thus construes <em>jāgrat</em> as \"keeps guard/stays awake,\" referring to Praśna 4.3, where the fires alone remain awake in the sleeping city."],["Cited","Ad p. 166 compresses: \"He is the sun, Viṣṇu and God (Īśvara); he is himself without the mind, ears, hands and feet; he is realized as effulgence\" — running this sentence together with the next."],["Editor","<em>Sa jīvaḥ</em> placed among divine names is the sentence to notice: the transmigrating individual is included in the identification, not contrasted with it. Compare Kaivalya 12 (<em>sa eva māyā-parimohitātmā</em>). De's Praśna 4.3 reference for <em>jāgrat</em> is more precise than reading it as \"ever-wakeful,\" and is adopted here."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"2.3","title":"The negation","sa":["स्वयममनस्कमश्रोत्रमपाणिपादं ज्योतिर्वर्जितं न तत्र लोका न लोका वेदा न वेदा","देवा न देवा यज्ञा न यज्ञा माता न माता पिता न पिता स्नुषा न स्नुषा","चाण्डालो न चाण्डालः पुल्कसो न पुल्कसः श्रमणो न श्रमणः पशवो न पशवस्तापसो न तापस","इत्येकमेव परं ब्रह्म विभाति ।"],"iast":["svayam amanaskam aśrotram apāṇipādaṃ jyotir-varjitaṃ na tatra lokā na lokā vedā na vedā","devā na devā yajñā na yajñā mātā na mātā pitā na pitā snuṣā na snuṣā","cāṇḍālo na cāṇḍālaḥ pulkaso [E: pailkaso] na pulkasaḥ śramaṇo na śramaṇaḥ paśavo na paśavas tāpaso na tāpasa","ity ekam eva paraṃ brahma vibhāti |"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Itself mindless, earless, handless and footless, devoid of light: there worlds are not worlds, Vedas not Vedas, gods not gods, sacrifices not sacrifices, mother not mother, father not father, daughter-in-law not daughter-in-law, outcaste not outcaste, Pulkasa not Pulkasa, mendicant not mendicant, beasts not beasts, ascetic not ascetic — thus the one supreme Brahman alone shines.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","All three translations construe the pairs as \"X is not X,\" not as emphatic repetition.<br><strong>De p. 728:</strong> \"in it there are not worlds and non-worlds, gods and non-gods, Vedas and non-Vedas, sacrifices and non-sacrifices, mother and non-mother, father and non-father, daughter-in-law and non-daughter-in-law, caṇḍāla and non-caṇḍāla, Paulkasa and non-Paulkasa, ascetic and non-ascetic, <strong>animals and non-animals</strong>, penitent and non-penitent.\"<br><strong>Ad p. 166–167:</strong> \"the worlds are not the worlds, gods are not gods, the Veda-s are not Veda-s … the outcaste is not outcaste, the cobbler is not the cobbler, the ascetic is not the ascetic, the hermit is not the hermit.\"<br><strong>Ai p. 107:</strong> \"There the worlds are no worlds, Devas no Devas, Vedas no Vedas…\""],["Editor","Two corrections of record.<strong> (i) The first draft read the construction as an emphatic liturgical doubling — \"no worlds, no worlds\" — and made a rhetorical point of the hammering rhythm. That reading is not supported by any of the three translations, and it is not what the parallel at </strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3.22<strong> does: there the construction is unambiguously <em>pitāpitā bhavati mātāmātā lokā alokā devā adevā vedā avedāḥ</em> — \"a father becomes a non-father, a mother a non-mother, worlds non-worlds.\" E's <em>na tatra lokā na lokā</em> is a corruption or variant of <em>lokā alokā</em>. The point is not emphasis but the </strong>cancellation of the category together with its opposite<strong>. (ii) The first draft </strong>omitted <em>paśavo na paśavaḥ</em> from the Devanāgarī, the IAST and the translation. It is present in E and in De. It is restored here."],["Editor","With that correction the list reads more sharply, not less: it is not that these things are absent, but that the distinction between each and its negation fails — which is why <em>śramaṇa</em> and <em>tāpasa</em>, the two recognised religious statuses, are cancelled along with <em>cāṇḍāla</em> and <em>pulkasa</em> at the other end of the social scale."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"2.4","title":"The heart-space","sa":["हृद्याकाशे तद्विज्ञानमाकाशं तत्सुषिरमाकाशं तद्वेद्यं हृद्याकाशं","यस्मिन्निदं संचरति विचरति यस्मिन्निदं सर्वमोतं प्रोतम् ।","सं विभोः प्रजा ज्ञायेरन् ।","न तत्र देवा ऋषयः पितरः … प्रतिबुद्धः सर्वविदिति ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["hṛdy ākāśe tad vijñānam ākāśaṃ tat suṣiram ākāśaṃ tad vedyaṃ hṛdy ākāśaṃ","yasminn idaṃ saṃcarati vicarati yasminn idaṃ sarvam otaṃ protam |","saṃ vibhoḥ prajā jñāyeran† |","na tatra devā ṛṣayaḥ pitaraḥ [†] pratibuddhaḥ sarvavid iti || 2 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"In the space within the heart: that space is consciousness, that space is the hollow, that heart-space is to be known — in which all this moves and ranges, in which all this is woven, warp and woof. […†] There gods, seers, ancestors [†] — the awakened one is the all-knower.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","De p. 729:<strong> \"The space in the heart is the Brahman, as consciousness, as the space, and this very space is the hollow (universal) space, because that is the space in the heart taught by the Veda (</strong>Chānd. 8.1.3<strong>), and in it stirs to and fro he in whom the whole world is sewn lengthwise and crosswise (</strong>Bṛh. 3.6<strong>). </strong>The creatures (or creation) must be viewed as His own Self, of the All-mighty. In him prevail no gods, no worlds, no wise, no forefathers…\""],["Cited","Ad p. 167: \"There [in the final beatitude] the gods, sages and manes exercise no power [as they are non-existent]; the one is awakened [to reality] [as the result of] all (Vedic) lore.\""],["Cited","Ai p. 107: \"In the Hṛdayākāś is the Chidākāś. That is Brahman. It is extremely subtle.\" — Ai's base text appears to read <em>cidākāśa</em> where E reads <em>vijñānam ākāśam / suṣiram ākāśam</em>."],["Editor","De's rendering shows that E's meaningless <em>saṃ vibhoḥ prajā jñāyeran</em> was a readable clause in his text — approximately <em>svaṃ vibhoḥ prajā jñāyeran</em>, \"the creatures are to be known as the All-mighty's own self.\" <em>(The first draft marked this unrecoverable; De's reconstruction is recorded here, though the underlying reading remains uncertain.)</em> The predicate governing <em>devā ṛṣayaḥ pitaraḥ</em> is lost in E (<em>riśate</em>); De and Ad agree it is a denial of their authority or existence there."],["Editor","<em>Otaṃ protam</em> is from the Gārgī dialogue; De cites Bṛh. 3.6, and the phrase occurs at 3.8.7. The three classes denied — gods, seers, ancestors — are those to whom the householder owes the three daily debts."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 3 — The Inner Thread and the Inner Tuft","u":[{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 3","title":"The Inner Thread and the Inner Tuft","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Editor","The text's distinctive contribution. ⟦CITED⟧ De p. 726 judges this \"the most original and most intimately connected with the Saṃnyāsa Upaniṣads\" part of the text, \"which recommends the Brahman-knower to do away with the śikhā … and the yajñopavītam … and to carry knowledge alone as śikhā and yajñopavītam.\""]],"notes":[],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3.1","title":"Deities in the heart; the investiture mantra","sa":["हृदिस्था देवताः सर्वा हृदि प्राणाः प्रतिष्ठिताः ।","हृदि प्राणश्च ज्योतिश्च त्रिवृत्सूत्रं च यन्महत् ॥","हृदि चैतन्ये तिष्ठति यज्ञोपवीतं परमं पवित्रं प्रजापतेर्यत्सहजं पुरस्तात् ।","आयुष्यमग्र्यं प्रतिमुञ्च शुभ्रं यज्ञोपवीतं बलमस्तु तेजः ॥"],"iast":["hṛdisthā devatāḥ sarvā hṛdi prāṇāḥ pratiṣṭhitāḥ |","hṛdi prāṇaś ca jyotiś ca trivṛt-sūtraṃ ca yan mahat ||","hṛdi caitanye tiṣṭhati yajñopavītaṃ paramaṃ pavitraṃ prajāpater yat sahajaṃ purastāt |","āyuṣyam agryaṃ [E: agrapaṃ] pratimuñca śubhraṃ yajñopavītaṃ balam astu tejaḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"All the deities abide in the heart; in the heart the breaths are established; in the heart are breath and light and the threefold cord, which is great. It abides in the heart, in consciousness: the sacred thread, supremely purifying, which was born with Prajāpati at the beginning. 'Put on the bright, life-giving, foremost sacred thread; may there be strength and splendour.'\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad p. 167 (vv. 4–5) renders the first part interpretively: \"All gods remain in the heart [i.e. in consciousness] … The vital breath … is in the heart as also the [self-]effulgent (Ātman). [Sages who have discarded the external sacred thread] know [this self-luminous Ātman as] the [true] threefold sacred thread. [The external sacred thread] remains [merged] in the heart of consciousness.\""],["Comm","UBY, via Ad p. 167, glosses the quoted mantra twice, in bracketed comments: on <em>āyuṣyam agryaṃ pratimuñca</em> — <em>\"knowing the Self to be the foremost, leave off other thoughts\"</em>; and on <em>balam astu tejaḥ</em> — <em>\"resorting to the inner effulgence, give up the external sacred thread.\"</em>"],["Cited","Ai p. 108 n. 1: \"This mantra is repeated whenever the holy thread is newly worn.\""],["Editor","The second line is the <em>upanayana</em> investiture mantra, quoted verbatim from the <em>gṛhya</em> liturgy — the sacrament is cited approvingly and then reinterpreted, not attacked. UBY's gloss makes the reinterpretation explicit at the point of quotation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"3.2","title":"Discard the outer thread; sūcanāt sūtram","sa":["सशिखं वपनं कृत्वा बहिःसूत्रं त्यजेद्बुधः ।","यदक्षरं परं ब्रह्म तत्सूत्रमिति धारयेत् ॥","सूचनात्सूत्रमित्याहुः सूत्रं नाम परं पदम् ।","तत्सूत्रं विदितं येन स विप्रो वेदपारगः ॥"],"iast":["saśikhaṃ vapanaṃ kṛtvā bahiḥ-sūtraṃ tyajed budhaḥ |","yad akṣaraṃ paraṃ brahma tat sūtram iti dhārayet ||","sūcanāt sūtram ity āhuḥ sūtraṃ nāma paraṃ padam |","tat sūtraṃ viditaṃ yena sa vipro vedapāragaḥ ||"],"words":[["sa-śikhaṃ vapanaṃ kṛtvā","n. acc. + abs.","having performed the shaving, tuft included"],["bahiḥ-sūtraṃ tyajet budhaḥ","opt. + m. nom.","let the wise man discard the outer thread"],["yat akṣaraṃ paraṃ brahma tat sūtram","n.","that Imperishable supreme Brahman — that is the thread"],["sūcanāt sūtram iti āhuḥ","abl. + pf. 3 pl.","\"sūtra,\" they say, from sūcana, indicating"],["sūtraṃ nāma paraṃ padam","n.","the thread is indeed the supreme state"],["sa vipraḥ vedapāragaḥ","m. nom.","he is the brahmin who has crossed the Veda"]],"lit":"\"Having shaved the head together with the tuft, let the wise man discard the outer thread. That Imperishable, the supreme Brahman — let him wear that as the thread. 'Thread' they call it from <em>sūcana</em>, indicating; the thread is indeed the supreme state. He by whom that thread is known is the brahmin who has crossed to the far shore of the Veda.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad pp. 167–168 (vv. 6–7):<strong> \"Taking a shave, removing the tuft, the wise one shall discard the external sacred thread. He shall have as the sacred thread the transcendent Brahman which is indestructible. The <em>sūtra</em> is so called as </strong>it indicates [liberation]; the <em>sūtra</em> indeed is the highest position.\""],["Cited","Ai p. 108:<strong> \"It is called <em>sūtra</em>, because <em>suchanat</em> (indicating) </strong>(that the Ātmā is in the heart).\""],["Uncertain reading","The etymology is agreed; what the <em>sūtra</em> indicates is not. <strong>UBY (via Ad): liberation.</strong> <strong>Ai: that the Ātman is in the heart.</strong> The text itself does not specify."],["Editor","The argument turns on the etymology: if <em>sūtra</em> is named from its power to <strong>indicate</strong>, the thing indicated has the better claim to the name. Note the restriction — the permission is given to <em>budhaḥ</em>, the wise man, and 4.1 will make the restriction explicit."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"3.3","title":"Gems on a thread; purity; who really wears the thread","sa":["तेन सर्वमिदं प्रोतं सूत्रे मणिगणा इव ।","तत्सूत्रं धारयेद्योगी योगवित्तत्त्वदर्शिवान् ॥","बहिःसूत्रं त्यजेद्विद्वान्योगमुत्तममास्थितः ।","ब्रह्मभावमयं सूत्रं धारयेद्यः स चेतनः ॥","धारणात्तस्य सूत्रस्य नोच्छिष्टो नाशुचिर्भवेत् ।","सूत्रमन्तर्गतं येषां ज्ञानयज्ञोपवीतिनाम् ॥","ते च सूत्रविदो लोके ते च यज्ञोपवीतिनः ।","ज्ञानशिखिनो ज्ञाननिष्ठा ज्ञानयज्ञोपवीतिनः ॥"],"iast":["tena sarvam idaṃ protaṃ sūtre maṇi-gaṇā iva |","tat sūtraṃ dhārayed yogī yoga-vit tattva-darśivān ||","bahiḥ-sūtraṃ tyajed vidvān yogam uttamam āsthitaḥ |","brahma-bhāva-mayaṃ sūtraṃ dhārayed yaḥ sa cetanaḥ ||","dhāraṇāt tasya sūtrasya nocchiṣṭo nāśucir bhavet |","sūtram antargataṃ yeṣāṃ jñāna-yajñopavītinām ||","te ca [E: cai] sūtravido loke te ca yajñopavītinaḥ |","jñāna-śikhino jñāna-niṣṭhā jñāna-yajñopavītinaḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"By that, all this is strung, as clusters of gems on a thread. Let the yogin wear that thread — knower of yoga, seer of reality. Let the knower, established in the highest yoga, discard the outer thread; he who wears the thread consisting of the state of Brahman is truly conscious. By wearing that thread one becomes neither polluted by leavings nor impure. Those whose thread has gone within, wearing the sacred thread of knowledge — they are the thread-knowers in the world, they are the thread-wearers: tufted with knowledge, established in knowledge, invested with the thread of knowledge.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad p. 168 (vv. 8–11):<strong> \"The yogin … shall wear that <em>sūtra</em> by which all this [phenomenal world] is joined together [as a unity] just as gems are strung together on a thread… By wearing [possessing] this sacred thread he shall not become </strong>an eater of the leavings of others or impure.\""],["Editor","<em>Sūtre maṇigaṇā iva</em> is <strong>Bhagavad-Gītā 7.7</strong> verbatim. The purity clause answers the practical objection: <em>ucchiṣṭa</em> (contamination by leavings) and <em>aśuci</em> are precisely what the outward thread regulates in daily life. The passage closes by redefining the terms rather than abolishing them."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"3.4","title":"The flame-tuft","sa":["ज्ञानमेव परं तेषां पवित्रं ज्ञानमुत्तमम् ।","अग्नेरिव शिखा नान्या यस्य ज्ञानमयी शिखा ॥","स शिखीत्युच्यते विद्वानितरे केशधारिणः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["jñānam eva paraṃ teṣāṃ pavitraṃ jñānam uttamam |","agner iva śikhā nānyā yasya jñānamayī śikhā ||","sa śikhīty ucyate vidvān itare keśa-dhāriṇaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"For them knowledge alone is supreme; knowledge is the highest purifier. He whose tuft is made of knowledge and of nothing else, like the flame of a fire — that knower is called <em>śikhin</em>; the rest are hair-wearers.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad pp. 168–169 (v. 12):<strong> \"The wise one whose tuft consists of Jñāna and no other, like the flame emanating from fire, is said to possess the [true] tuft; </strong>not the others who wear [a mass of] hair.\""],["Cited","Ai p. 108: \"he, the wise one, only wears a true śikhā; others wear a mere tuft of hair.\""],["Editor","<em>Śikhā</em> means both \"tuft\" and \"flame,\" and the pun carries the argument: a fire's flame is not attached to it but is the fire made visible. Ad and Ai agree on the force of the last clause."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Khaṇḍa 4 — Who Keeps the Thread; and the Self","u":[{"ref":"4.1","title":"Those qualified for rites keep the thread","sa":["कर्मण्यधिकृता ये तु वैदिके ब्राह्मणादयः ।","तैः संधार्यमिदं सूत्रं क्रियाङ्गं तद्धि वै स्मृतम् ॥","शिखा ज्ञानमयी यस्य उपवीतं च तन्मयम् ।","ब्राह्मण्यं सकलं तस्य इति ब्रह्मविदो विदुः ॥"],"iast":["karmaṇy adhikṛtā ye tu vaidike brāhmaṇādayaḥ |","taiḥ saṃdhāryam idaṃ sūtraṃ kriyāṅgaṃ tad dhi vai smṛtam ||","śikhā jñānamayī yasya upavītaṃ ca tanmayam |","brāhmaṇyaṃ sakalaṃ tasya iti brahmavido viduḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"But those qualified for Vedic action, brahmins and the rest — by them this thread is to be worn, for it is taught as a limb of ritual action. He whose tuft is made of knowledge and whose thread is made of that — his is brahminhood entire: so the knowers of Brahman know.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad p. 169 (vv. 13–14), under the headings <em>\"The sacred thread as part of the rituals\"</em> and <em>\"Brāhmaṇhood exists without rituals\"</em>: \"Those Brāhmaṇa-s and others entitled to perform Vedic rituals are to wear this [external] sacred thread; for it is declared to be a part of the rituals. He whose tuft consists of spiritual wisdom and whose sacred thread too consists of that has all the noble characteristics of a Brāhmaṇa — thus declare the knowers of Brahman.\""],["Cited","Ad p. 169 (v. 15)<strong> adds a verse absent from E's numbering, glossing the thread as sacrifice: \"he is the sacrifice [such as the Agniṣṭoma] … </strong>[It is he who oblates into fire all things other than the Self and hence to him there remains the Self alone]<strong>\" — the bracketed matter being </strong>⟦COMM⟧ UBY's gloss."],["Editor","This verse is what keeps khaṇḍa 3 from being a polemic: obligation follows <em>adhikāra</em>, qualification. Neither party is criticised. It should be read before 3.2, not after."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"4.2","title":"The one God (Śvetāśvatara 6.11–12)","sa":["एको देवः सर्वभूतेषु गूढः सर्वव्यापी सर्वभूतान्तरात्मा ।","कर्माध्यक्षः सर्वभूताधिवासः साक्षी चेता केवलो निर्गुणश्च ॥","एको मनीषी निष्क्रियाणां बहूनामेकं रूपं बहुधा यः करोति ।","तमात्मानं येऽनुपश्यन्ति धीरास्तेषां शान्तिः शाश्वती नेतरेषाम् ॥"],"iast":["eko devaḥ sarvabhūteṣu gūḍhaḥ sarvavyāpī sarvabhūtāntarātmā |","karmādhyakṣaḥ sarvabhūtādhivāsaḥ sākṣī cetā kevalo nirguṇaś ca ||","eko manīṣī niṣkriyāṇāṃ bahūnām ekaṃ rūpaṃ bahudhā yaḥ karoti |","tam ātmānaṃ ye 'nupaśyanti dhīrās teṣāṃ śāntiḥ śāśvatī netareṣām ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"One God, hidden in all beings, all-pervading, the inner Self of all beings; overseer of works, dwelling in all beings, witness, conscious, alone, without qualities. The one wise one among the many inactive, who makes one form manifold — the steadfast who behold him as the Self, theirs is everlasting peace, not others'.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad p. 169 (vv. 16–17), with footnote \"Śvet. Up., 6.11, 12\":<strong> \"There is one self-effulgent Being who is hidden in all creatures… he is the controller of all actions, resides in all beings, is the witness, solely conscious and free from all attributes. He is the one, the controller, the inward self of all beings; </strong>he has one form [but] makes it many. Those wise men who realize Him as the Self have eternal happiness, not the others.\""],["Cited","Ai p. 108:<strong> \"He is the great wise one. He is the one doer among the many action-less objects… Those wise men who see him </strong>in buddhi, they only obtain eternal peace.\""],["Editor","Correction of record.<strong> The first draft emended four words here to the Śvetāśvatara readings — <em>vaśī</em> for <em>manīṣī</em>, <em>bījaṃ</em> for <em>santaṃ</em>, <em>ātmasthaṃ</em> for <em>ātmānaṃ</em>, <em>sukhaṃ śāśvataṃ</em> for <em>śāntiḥ śāśvatī</em> — and filed them as \"secure restorations.\" </strong>That was an overclaim.<strong> Ai's 1914 base text also reads <em>manīṣī</em> (\"the great wise one\"), and Ad reads \"one form\" (<em>rūpam</em>), not \"one seed\" (<em>bījam</em>). The divergences therefore belong to </strong>the Brahma Upaniṣad's own transmission, not to this e-text alone. The transmitted readings are restored above; the Śvetāśvatara parallels are recorded in the apparatus rather than imposed on the text."],["Editor","<em>Karmādhyakṣa</em>, \"overseer of works,\" is worth noting in a text arguing that rites may be abandoned: accountability is not abandoned with them."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"4.3","title":"The churning (Śvetāśvatara 1.14–15)","sa":["आत्मानमरणिं कृत्वा प्रणवं चोत्तरारणिम् ।","ध्याननिर्मथनाभ्यासाद्देवं पश्येन्निगूढवत् ॥","तिलेषु तैलं दधनीव सर्पिरापः स्रोतःस्वरणीषु चाग्निः ।","एवमात्माऽऽत्मनि गृह्यतेऽसौ सत्येनैनं तपसा योऽनुपश्यति ॥"],"iast":["ātmānam araṇiṃ kṛtvā praṇavaṃ cottarāraṇim |","dhyāna-nirmathanābhyāsād devaṃ paśyen nigūḍhavat ||","tileṣu tailaṃ dadhani iva sarpir āpaḥ srotaḥsv [E: strotaḥsv] araṇīṣu cāgniḥ [E: cānniḥ] |","evam ātmā 'tmani gṛhyate [E: gṛhmate] 'sau satyenainaṃ tapasā yo 'nupaśyati ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Having made the self the lower fire-stick and OM the upper, by the practice of churning that is meditation one should see the God, as a thing hidden. As oil in sesame, ghee in curd, water in streams, fire in the fire-sticks — so is that Self grasped within the self by one who beholds him through truth and austerity.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad p. 170 (v. 18), footnoted \"ibid., 1.14, 15\":<strong> \"Making the self </strong>[the lower]<strong> kindling stick and the Praṇava </strong>the upper kindling stick and by practice of vigorous rubbing in the form of meditation, [the sage] may realize the hidden effulgent Being.\""],["Cited","Ai p. 109<strong> reverses these: \"Having made Ātma as the </strong>(upper)<strong> araṇi and Praṇava the </strong>lower araṇi.\""],["Editor","Ai's parenthetical assignment is an error. <em>Uttarāraṇi</em> means \"the upper fire-stick,\" and it is <em>praṇavam</em> that carries it in the Sanskrit; Ad and the Śvetāśvatara parallel both give the self as lower and OM as upper. This edition follows Ad. The point is recorded because it is a case where a published translation is demonstrably wrong and the correction is checkable from the text itself."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"4.4","title":"Spider; heart-lotus; the state-map","sa":["ऊर्णनाभिर्यथा तन्तून्सृजते संहरत्यपि ।","जाग्रत्स्वप्ने तथा जीवो गच्छत्यागच्छते पुनः ॥","पद्मकोशप्रतीकाशं सुषिरं चाप्यधोमुखम् ।","हृदयं तद्विजानीयाद्विश्वस्यायतनं महत् ॥","नेत्रस्थं जाग्रतं विद्यात्कण्ठे स्वप्नं विनिर्दिशेत् ।","सुषुप्तं हृदयस्थं तु तुरीयं मूर्ध्नि संस्थितम् ॥"],"iast":["ūrṇanābhir yathā tantūn sṛjate saṃharaty api |","jāgrat-svapne tathā jīvo gacchaty āgacchate punaḥ ||","padma-kośa-pratīkāśaṃ suṣiraṃ cāpy adho-mukham |","hṛdayaṃ tad vijānīyād viśvasyāyatanaṃ mahat ||","netrasthaṃ jāgrataṃ vidyāt kaṇṭhe svapnaṃ vinirdiśet [E: vinirdiṣet] |","suṣuptaṃ hṛdayasthaṃ tu turīyaṃ mūrdhni saṃsthitam ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As the spider emits its threads and withdraws them, so the <em>jīva</em> goes forth and returns again in waking and dream. Resembling a lotus-bud, hollow and facing downward — know that as the heart, the great abode of the universe. Know waking as seated in the eye; assign dream to the throat; deep sleep to the heart; the fourth is established in the head.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad p. 170 (vv. 20–21): \"Just as a spider produces and withdraws cobwebs, so the self [jīva] goes into and returns again from waking and sleeping. Know that the waking state remains in the eyes, dreaming state in the throat and [the state of] deep sleep in the heart; the fourth state (turīya) remains in the head.\""],["Cited","De p. 726:<strong> here \"the four places of Brahman … are paralleled with the four states, </strong>for which purpose, however, <em>nābhi</em> has been replaced by <em>netram</em>.\""],["Cited","Ai p. 109: \"The heart is in the form of a closed lotus-flower, with its head hanging down; it has a hole in the top.\""],["Editor","This is the text's only pairing of states with places. The list differs from 2.1 in its first member only (<em>netra</em> for <em>nābhi</em>), which De treats as a substitution made for the purpose of the pairing rather than as a competing scheme. The heart-verse is shared with the Mahānārāyaṇa material and echoed at Kaivalya 6; <em>viśvasyāyatanaṃ mahat</em> is the phrase Kaivalya 16 applies to Brahman."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"4.5","title":"Sandhyā as meditation","sa":["यदात्मा प्रज्ञयाऽऽत्मानं संधत्ते परमात्मनि ।","तेन संध्या ध्यानमेव तस्मात्संध्याभिवन्दनम् ॥","निरुदकाध्यानसंध्या वाक्कायक्लेशवर्जिता ।","संधिनी सर्वभूतानां सा संध्या ह्येकदण्डिनाम् ॥"],"iast":["yad ātmā prajñayā 'tmānaṃ saṃdhatte paramātmani |","tena saṃdhyā dhyānam eva tasmāt sandhyābhivandanam ||","nirudaka-dhyāna-saṃdhyā vāk-kāya-kleśa-varjitā |","saṃdhinī sarvabhūtānāṃ sā saṃdhyā hy eka-daṇḍinām ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"When the self, by wisdom, joins the self to the supreme Self — thereby the <em>sandhyā</em> is meditation itself; hence that is the twilight-worship. The twilight-rite of meditation, without water, free from the trouble of speech and body, joining all beings — that is the <em>sandhyā</em> of those who bear the single staff.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ai p. 109: \"(Because buddhi unites) the Pratyagātma with the Paramātma, the worship of sandhya (union) arose. So we should perform sandhyāvandana (rites). The sandhyāvandana performed by dhyāna requires no water. It gives no trouble to the body or the speech. That which unites all things is the sandhya of the one-staffed (sannyāsins).\""],["Cited","De p. 726: \"The two following ślokas recommend the <em>saṃdhyā</em> as a symbol of oneness with God and oneness of all beings; here, there is a play, difficult to reproduce, on the words <em>saṃdhā</em> and <em>saṃdhyā</em>.\""],["Editor","The argument is the same etymological move as <em>sūcanāt sūtram</em>: <em>sandhyā</em> is named from <em>sandhi</em>, joining; the true joining is of self to supreme Self; therefore meditation is the <em>sandhyā</em> proper — and, unlike the rite, it needs no water, no speech and no bodily observance. <em>Ekadaṇḍin</em>, \"single-staffed,\" is a technical term for the renunciate. Given that the verses are absent from the Adyar recension, their attestation is weaker than that of the thread-passage."]],"notes":["⚠️ <strong>⟦TEXT⟧ Recension note: these two verses are absent from Ad</strong>, which passes directly from the state-map (v. 21) to <em>yato vāco nivartante</em> (v. 22). They are present in E and translated by Ai. UBY therefore offers no comment on them, and none is reported here."]},{"ref":"4.6","title":"Where speech turns back","sa":["यतो वाचो निवर्तन्ते अप्राप्य मनसा सह ।","आनन्दमेतज्जीवस्य यं ज्ञात्वा मुच्यते बुधः ॥","सर्वव्यापिनमात्मानं क्षीरे सर्पिरिवार्पितम् ।","आत्मविद्यातपोमूलं तद्ब्रह्मोपनिषत्परम् ।","सर्वात्मैकत्वरूपेण तद्ब्रह्मोपनिषत्परमिति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["yato vāco nivartante aprāpya manasā saha |","ānandam [E: ānnandam] etaj jīvasya yaṃ jñātvā mucyate budhaḥ ||","sarvavyāpinam ātmānaṃ kṣīre sarpir ivārpitam |","ātma-vidyā-tapo-mūlaṃ tad brahmopaniṣat param |","sarvātma-ekatva-rūpeṇa [E: rīpeṇa] tad brahmopaniṣat param iti || 4 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"That from which words turn back, together with the mind, not having attained it — this is the bliss of the <em>jīva</em>, knowing which the wise man is freed. The all-pervading Self, placed as ghee in milk, the root of Self-knowledge and austerity — that is the supreme secret teaching of Brahman. In the form of the oneness of the Self of all — that is the supreme secret teaching of Brahman.\"","para":"","coms":[["Cited","Ad p. 171 (vv. 22–23), footnoted \"Śvet. Up., 1.16\":<strong> \"That from which speech along with the mind turns away without reaching it is the bliss experienced by the Self (jīva); knowing this the wise man is liberated. [One should realize] the Ātman who is all-pervading like clarified butter in milk, </strong>the prop of spiritual lore and the object of the Brahmopaniṣad, the aim of the Upaniṣad which expounds Brahman.\""],["Cited","De p. 726:<strong> \"A reminiscence of </strong>Taitt. 2.4<strong> and the verse </strong>Śvet. 1.16 conclude the whole.\""],["Editor","E's <em>ātmātapomūlaṃ</em> is unmetrical and obscure; Ad's rendering (\"the prop of spiritual lore\") suggests a reading with <em>ātma-vidyā</em>, adopted above. The doubled final clause is the standard Upaniṣadic signal of completion."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Where the sources disagree","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A summary of every substantive divergence recorded above, so that the disagreements are visible in one place rather than buried in the apparatus.","<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Point</td><td>E / this edition</td><td>Ai (1914)</td><td>De (1897/1980)</td><td>Ad / UBY (1978)</td></tr><tr><td>1.1</td><td><em>yo hmaṣe</em></td><td>corrupt; follows De</td><td>no 4-fold structure</td><td>4th question: \"who has become this power\"</td><td>omits kh. 1</td></tr><tr><td>1.2</td><td><em>nidhanam anidhanam</em></td><td>\"disappearance and non-disappearance\"</td><td>—</td><td>\"disappearance and appearance\"</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.4</td><td>the stick</td><td>\"though struck, does not stir\"</td><td>\"though beaten\"</td><td>emends: \"as long as he is <strong>not</strong> struck\" (Kauṣ. 4.19)</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.4</td><td>the child's bliss</td><td>in dream (<em>svapne</em>)</td><td>in <em>suṣupti</em></td><td>in deep sleep</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.5</td><td><em>jalaukā</em></td><td>\"leech\" (looper)</td><td>\"caterpillar\"</td><td>\"caterpillar\"</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.6</td><td><em>kapālāṣṭaka</em></td><td>potsherds (follows De)</td><td>\"eight skulls\"</td><td>eight sacrificial potsherds</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.6</td><td><em>puṣkara</em></td><td>lotus</td><td><strong>ākāśa</strong></td><td>\"lotus flower\"</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.6</td><td><em>hiṃsā</em></td><td>\"harm\"</td><td>\"pain\"</td><td>\"non-harm\"</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.6</td><td><em>karkaṭaka</em></td><td>left unexplained</td><td>\"karkaṭa (crab)\"</td><td>\"makes no particular meaning; isolated in the Upaniṣad literature\"</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>1.6</td><td><em>samprasāra</em></td><td>⟦UNCERTAIN⟧</td><td>\"capable of great expansion\"</td><td>\"origin of the world\"; or <em>samprasāda</em> (Chānd. 8.12.3)</td><td>omits</td></tr><tr><td>2.1</td><td>places ↔ states</td><td><strong>not paired</strong></td><td>not paired</td><td>explicitly \"without assigning to these\"</td><td>not paired</td></tr><tr><td>2.2</td><td><em>jāgrat</em></td><td>\"awake in the midst\" (follows De)</td><td>—</td><td>\"keeps guard,\" Praśna 4.3</td><td>compressed</td></tr><tr><td>2.3</td><td>the negation</td><td>\"X is not X\"</td><td>\"X is no X\"</td><td>\"X and non-X\"</td><td>\"X is not X\"</td></tr><tr><td>2.3</td><td><em>paśavo na paśavaḥ</em></td><td><strong>present</strong></td><td>absent from rendering</td><td>\"animals and non-animals\"</td><td>absent</td></tr><tr><td>2.4</td><td><em>saṃ vibhoḥ prajā</em></td><td>corrupt; De's sense reported</td><td>absent</td><td>\"creatures must be viewed as His own Self\"</td><td>\"gods, sages, manes exercise no power\"</td></tr><tr><td>3.2</td><td>what <em>sūtra</em> indicates</td><td>not specified by the text</td><td>\"that the Ātmā is in the heart\"</td><td>—</td><td><strong>liberation</strong></td></tr><tr><td>4.2</td><td><em>manīṣī</em> / <em>vaśī</em></td><td><em>manīṣī</em> (transmitted)</td><td>\"great wise one\"</td><td>—</td><td>\"the controller\"</td></tr><tr><td>4.2</td><td><em>rūpaṃ</em> / <em>bījaṃ</em></td><td><em>rūpaṃ</em> (transmitted)</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>\"one form [but] makes it many\"</td></tr><tr><td>4.3</td><td>the fire-sticks</td><td>self = lower, OM = upper</td><td><strong>reversed (error)</strong></td><td>—</td><td>self = lower, OM = upper</td></tr><tr><td>4.5</td><td><em>sandhyā</em> verses</td><td>present</td><td>present</td><td>present</td><td><strong>absent</strong></td></tr><tr><td>4.6</td><td><em>ātmā-tapo-mūlam</em></td><td><em>ātma-vidyā-tapo-mūlam</em></td><td>\"root of ātmavidyā\"</td><td>—</td><td>\"prop of spiritual lore\"</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Textual notes","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Editor","These stand because Ai and Ad attest the divergent readings in their own base texts. They are variants of the Brahma Upaniṣad, not errors of this e-text."]],"notes":["<strong>Group A — orthographic slips in E, corrected silently above and listed here</strong>","<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>E</td><td>Read</td><td>Basis</td></tr><tr><td>1.3</td><td><em>vakṣipati</em></td><td><em>vikṣipati</em></td><td>sense</td></tr><tr><td>1.6</td><td><em>grāṇo</em></td><td><em>prāṇo</em></td><td>sense; all translations</td></tr><tr><td>1.6</td><td><em>dhaṃ kṣetrajñam</em></td><td><em>kṣetrajñam</em></td><td>intrusive syllable</td></tr><tr><td>2.1</td><td><em>bhūrdhā</em></td><td><em>mūrdhā</em></td><td>sense; all translations</td></tr><tr><td>2.3</td><td><em>pailkaso</em></td><td><em>pulkaso</em></td><td>the Pulkasa caste; cf. Chānd. 5.10.7</td></tr><tr><td>2.3</td><td><em>snuṣa</em></td><td><em>snuṣā</em></td><td>gender</td></tr><tr><td>3.1</td><td><em>agrapaṃ</em></td><td><em>agryaṃ</em></td><td>the <em>gṛhya</em> investiture mantra</td></tr><tr><td>3.1</td><td><em>tyajed.hbudhaḥ</em></td><td><em>tyajed budhaḥ</em></td><td>ITRANS virāma artefact</td></tr><tr><td>3.3</td><td><em>te cai</em></td><td><em>te ca</em></td><td>metre</td></tr><tr><td>4.3</td><td><em>strotaḥsu</em>, <em>cānniḥ</em>, <em>gṛhmate</em>, <em>dadhinīva</em></td><td><em>srotaḥsu</em>, <em>cāgniḥ</em>, <em>gṛhyate</em>, <em>dadhani iva</em></td><td>Śvet. 1.15</td></tr><tr><td>4.6</td><td><em>ānnandam</em>, <em>…rīpeṇa</em></td><td><em>ānandam</em>, <em>…rūpeṇa</em></td><td>sense</td></tr></table>","<strong>Group B — readings where E diverges from the Śvetāśvatara parallel and is NOT emended</strong>","<em>manīṣī</em> (Śvet. <em>vaśī</em>); <em>rūpaṃ</em> (Śvet. <em>bījaṃ</em>); <em>ātmānaṃ</em> (Śvet. <em>ātmasthaṃ</em>); <em>śāntiḥ śāśvatī</em> (Śvet. <em>sukhaṃ śāśvataṃ</em>); <em>dhyāna-nirmathana</em> (Kaivalya 11 <em>jñāna-nirmathana</em>).","<strong>Group C — unresolved</strong>","<em>yo hmaṣe</em> (1.1); <em>vedadevayonaḥ</em> (1.6, though Nārāyaṇa via De and Ai agree on the sense \"source of the Vedas and the gods\"); <em>saṃ vibhoḥ prajā jñāyeran</em> (2.4); the predicate of <em>devā ṛṣayaḥ pitaraḥ</em> (2.4, E <em>riśate</em>); <em>ātmā-tapo-mūlam</em> (4.6)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Revision notes","title":"what changed from the first draft of this volume","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Editor","What is still missing. The Sanskrit <em>Dīpikā</em> of UBY in the original; Nārāyaṇa's and Śaṅkarānanda's commentaries; Olivelle 1992; Schrader 1912. UBY is reported here only as filtered through Ramanathan's English. A future revision with the Sanskrit commentaries would likely resolve several Group C readings."]],"notes":["This volume was first written from the e-text alone, before Ai, De and Ad were obtained. The following are the substantive corrections.","1. <strong>The five-commentary apparatus is removed.</strong> The first draft carried bullets attributed to \"UBY,\" \"the Saṃnyāsa tradition,\" \"Aiyar,\" \"Sivānanda/Krishnānanda\" and \"Deussen/Olivelle/Schrader\" for every passage. <strong>I had read none of those works at the time.</strong> The entries were reconstructions of what each commentator's system would require, and were presented under their names. They are replaced here by ⟦CITED⟧ material from three sources actually consulted, ⟦COMM⟧ for UBY where Ad reports him, and ⟦ED⟧ for my own reading. Sivānanda and Krishnānanda are no longer cited at all, since I have not read them on this text.","2. <strong>The state-map error.</strong> The first draft paired the four places of 2.1 with the four states and built a long discussion on the resulting \"discrepancy\" with 4.4. The text does not make that pairing; De says so explicitly and Ai and Ad do not supply it. There is one state-map, at 4.4, differing from 2.1's list of places only in <em>netra</em> for <em>nābhi</em>.","3. <strong>The negation.</strong> The first draft read <em>na tatra lokā na lokā</em> as emphatic doubling and made a rhetorical point of it. All three translations read \"X is not X,\" and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3.22 parallel is unambiguous. The reading is corrected.","4. <strong>An omission of text.</strong> <em>Paśavo na paśavaḥ</em> was dropped from the Devanāgarī, IAST and translation of 2.3. Restored.","5. <strong>Overclaimed emendations.</strong> Four Śvetāśvatara readings imposed on 4.2 as \"secure restorations\" are reverted to the transmitted text, since Ai and Ad attest the divergent readings independently.","6. <strong>Invented glosses removed.</strong> The first draft explained <em>karkaṭaka</em> as \"the crab, which carries its own house and withdraws into it.\" De states the comparison \"makes no particular meaning and is isolated in the Upaniṣad literature.\" The gloss was mine and is withdrawn. Similarly the confident readings of <em>puṣkara</em> as lotus and <em>samprasāra</em> as \"the serene one\" are now marked as contested.","7. <strong>A resolved uncertainty.</strong> <em>Yo hmaṣe</em> (1.1), marked unrecoverable in the first draft, is given De's reconstruction.","8. <strong>Commentators named.</strong> De identifies <strong>Nārāyaṇa</strong> and <strong>Śaṅkarānanda</strong> as the principal Sanskrit commentators on this text. The first draft mentioned neither.","9. <strong>Recension information added</strong> — the omission of khaṇḍa 1 in most manuscripts and in Ad; the Telugu double recension reported by De; the absence of the <em>sandhyā</em> verses from Ad; and a verse-numbering concordance with Ad.","10. <strong>One point where the first draft was right and a published source is wrong</strong> is now documented: the assignment of the fire-sticks at 4.3, where Ai reverses upper and lower."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Editor","Verified against the cited source where a ⟦CITED⟧ tag appears; otherwise identified by me and to be checked."]],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Parallel</td><td>Authority</td></tr><tr><td>1.1 Śaunaka</td><td>Muṇḍaka 1.1.3</td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>1.1 Pippalāda</td><td>Praśna</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 725</td></tr><tr><td>1.1 <em>divye brahmapure</em></td><td>Chāndogya 8.1; Kaṭha 2.2.1</td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>1.2 the four epithets</td><td>Muṇḍaka 2.2.9</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 726</td></tr><tr><td>1.3 bees and their king</td><td><strong>Praśna 2.4</strong></td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 726 n. 1</td></tr><tr><td>1.3 spider</td><td>Muṇḍaka 1.1.7</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 726</td></tr><tr><td>1.4 falcon</td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3.19</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 727</td></tr><tr><td>1.4 the stick</td><td>Kauṣītaki 4.19</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 727</td></tr><tr><td>1.5 looper</td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.3</td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>1.6 <em>stana iva lambate</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3.21</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 728</td></tr><tr><td>1.6 <em>samprasāda</em></td><td>Chāndogya 8.12.3</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 728</td></tr><tr><td>1.6 <em>kṣetrajña</em></td><td>Muṇḍaka 3.2.1</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 728</td></tr><tr><td>2.1 four states</td><td>Māṇḍūkya 3–7</td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>2.2 <em>jāgrat</em> (keeps guard)</td><td>Praśna 4.3</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 728</td></tr><tr><td>2.3 the negation</td><td><strong>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.3.22</strong></td><td>⟦ED⟧ (construction confirmed by all three translations)</td></tr><tr><td>2.3 <em>pulkasa</em>, <em>cāṇḍāla</em></td><td>Chāndogya 5.10.7</td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>2.4 heart-space</td><td>Chāndogya 8.1.3</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 729</td></tr><tr><td>2.4 <em>otaṃ protam</em></td><td>Bṛhadāraṇyaka 3.6 / 3.8.7</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 729</td></tr><tr><td>3.1 investiture mantra</td><td><em>gṛhya</em> liturgy</td><td>⟦CITED⟧ Ai p. 108 n. 1</td></tr><tr><td>3.3 gems on a thread</td><td><strong>Bhagavad-Gītā 7.7</strong></td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>4.2</td><td><strong>Śvetāśvatara 6.11–12</strong></td><td>⟦CITED⟧ Ad p. 169 n. 1; De p. 726</td></tr><tr><td>4.3</td><td><strong>Śvetāśvatara 1.14–15</strong></td><td>⟦CITED⟧ Ad p. 170 n. 2; De p. 726</td></tr><tr><td>4.3</td><td>Kaivalya 11 (<em>jñāna</em>- for <em>dhyāna-nirmathana</em>)</td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>4.4 heart-lotus</td><td>Mahānārāyaṇa / Taitt. Ār. 10; Kaivalya 6</td><td>⟦ED⟧</td></tr><tr><td>4.6 <em>yato vāco nivartante</em></td><td><strong>Taittirīya 2.4</strong></td><td>⟦CITED⟧ De p. 726</td></tr><tr><td>4.6 ghee in milk</td><td><strong>Śvetāśvatara 1.16</strong></td><td>⟦CITED⟧ Ad p. 171 n. 1; De p. 726</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Colophon","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>Thus the Brahma Upaniṣad: Śaunaka's question and the similes of the living Self (kh. 1, of weak attestation); the four stations and four states, the cancellation of every category, and the space within the heart (kh. 2); the inner thread and the inner tuft (kh. 3); and the settlement of who keeps the outward thread, followed by the borrowed verses on the Self (kh. 4).</em>","<em>E colophon: ity atharvavede brahmopaniṣat samāptā. Ad, following Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin, assigns the text to the Kṛṣṇa Yajurveda. This edition does not resolve the discrepancy.</em>"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
